Inversion definition: for key with >=2 events, all of which have parseable ts... Actually handle: 
- dated = events with _ts not None; if len(dated) < 2 → "undated_keys" (can't compare). 
- arrival winner = max line among ALL events (including undated; arrival order doesn't need timestamps).
- time winner = max _ts, tie → max line among those.
- If arrival winner's _ts is None → undated key, skip.
- If arrival._ts == time._ts → tie (the time winner chosen by line order equals arrival winner by construction) → count tie_resolved, no inversion. Actually if arrival._ts == max_ts, then arrival is among ties and tie-break picks max line = arrival winner → same event. So no inversion.
- Else compare payloads: if equal → order_only_inversion else state_changing_inversion.
- Also record direction: arrival created_at vs time created_at: always arrival_ts < time_ts in an inversion? If arrival._ts < time._ts yes. Since time winner has max ts and arrival has less, yes strictly less. Record delta_seconds.

Additional: "future-dated" flag for the time winner: created_at > _received_at or created_at > pinned_at.

Census:
- `future_vs_received`: created_at > _received_at (ts diff > 0); max skew; counts by actor/type; note missing _received_at count.
- `future_vs_pin`: created_at > pinned_at.
- `clock_skew_histogram` maybe buckets.

